    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A squeak tells you two surfaces are rubbing. A thump tells you something is hitting
          something, on a schedule — and that schedule is the most useful piece of evidence you
          will get. Most thumping treadmills can be diagnosed in ten minutes with a strip of tape
          and a watch, before a single bolt is turned.
        </p>

        <div id="thump-vs-squeak" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. A Thump Is Not a Squeak</h2>
<p>
            It is worth separating the two at the start, because the fixes barely overlap. A
            squeak or a creak is friction: metal on metal at a loose joint, a dry belt on a dry
            deck, a bearing running short of grease. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squeaking noise guide</a>
            covers that family, and if what you hear is high-pitched and continuous, start there.
          </p>
          <p>
            A thump, knock or clunk is an impact. Something with mass is moving and then stopping
            against something else — a raised seam hitting a roller, a deck dropping onto a
            hardened cushion, a frame rocking onto a short foot, an incline motor taking up slack in
            its mounting. Impacts are periodic. They happen once per revolution of whatever is
            causing them, or once per footstrike, or once per incline change, and that periodicity
            is what makes them easier to locate than a squeak, not harder.
          </p>
          <p>
            The working vocabulary in this guide is loose but useful. A <strong>thump</strong> is
            soft and low, usually from rubber or a belt. A <strong>knock</strong> is sharper and
            usually metallic, from something with play in it. A <strong>clunk</strong> is a single
            heavier event, often from the incline system or a frame shifting. Do not get attached to
            the label you chose; get attached to the timing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timing-the-thump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Time It Against the Belt and Your Stride</h2>
<p>
            Every thump on a treadmill keeps one of three tempos, and telling them apart is the
            whole technique.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Slow and steady, with nobody on the machine.</strong> A thump every few seconds
            at walking speed is almost always the running belt itself, because the belt is the
            slowest-rotating part of the machine. On a typical home treadmill one full revolution of
            the belt at 2 mph takes roughly three to four seconds. Double the speed and the thump
            comes twice as often.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>At your stride rate, only when you are on it.</strong> A thump that lands each
            time a foot lands, and vanishes when you step off and let the belt run empty, is
            structural. The belt and rollers are not the cause; the deck, its mounts, the frame or
            the floor are.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Fast, closer to a rumble or a beat.</strong> Rollers and the motor turn many
            times per second, so a defect there rarely produces a distinct thump. It produces a
            wobble, a throb or a rhythmic knock that blurs into a hum as speed rises.
          </p>
          <p>
            To confirm the first case, unplug the machine and put a short strip of painter's tape
            across the top of the belt near one edge, or mark the edge with chalk. Plug back in,
            stand on the side rails rather than the belt, start it at the lowest speed, and watch
            the mark come round. If the thump arrives each time the mark reaches the same point —
            usually as it passes over the front or rear roller — the cause is on the belt, and you
            now know roughly where. Move the tape until it sits on the thump and you have found it.
            Keep hands and loose clothing well clear of the rollers while the belt is moving, and
            stop it before you reach for anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then the second test: walk slowly on the machine and count. If the thump lands with
            your feet rather than with the tape, you are in structural territory. If it does both
            — a once-per-revolution thump that gets louder under your weight — the belt is still the
            cause and your weight is simply pressing it harder into the deck.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt-seam" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Once Per Revolution: The Belt Seam</h2>
<p>
            Every treadmill belt is a flat strip joined into a loop, and the joint is the seam.
            On a good belt it is nearly invisible and makes no noise. On an old, poorly made or
            heat-damaged belt it lifts, stiffens or thickens, and a stiff seam bending over a
            two-inch roller makes a soft thud every time it goes round.
          </p>
          <p>
            Unplug the machine, find the seam with your hand, and inspect it in raking light. You
            are looking for a ridge you can feel with a fingertip, a corner of the joint that has
            started to peel, or a seam that has gone hard and glassy compared with the rest of the
            belt. Check the underside too: some seams are fine on top and lifting on the fabric
            side.
          </p>
          <p>
            A brand-new belt often has a slightly noticeable seam for its first few hours, which
            usually settles as the belt beds in. A seam that thumps on an old belt does not settle;
            it gets worse, and a seam that is visibly separating is a belt to replace rather than
            to nurse. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-often-should-a-treadmill-belt-be-replaced/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">when a treadmill belt is due</a>
            covers the other signs, and
            <a href="/how-to-replace-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to replace a treadmill belt</a>
            covers the job itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            One complication worth knowing: a seam noise that appeared shortly after someone
            over-tightened the belt is sometimes the tension, not the seam. A belt pulled too tight
            bends the joint harder over the rollers. Check the lift test in our
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt tension guide</a>
            before ordering anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="damaged-belt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Once Per Revolution: A Lump, Bulge or Damaged Belt</h2>
<p>
            If the seam is clean but the tape still lands on the thump somewhere else, look at that
            section of belt closely, both sides.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Delamination.</strong> The running surface separating from the fabric backing
            produces a bubble or a soft lump, and a bubble passing over a roller thumps. This is the
            one belt fault that means stop using the machine rather than plan a repair, because a
            delaminating belt can tear or bunch suddenly underfoot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Something stuck to the underside.</strong> A blob of hardened lubricant, a
            piece of tape, chewing gum carried in on a shoe, or a wad of compacted carpet fibre
            all form a lump that hits the rollers once per revolution. Cleaning it off with the
            belt slackened usually ends the noise completely, and it is the most satisfying fix on
            this page.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A crease or a set.</strong> Belts on machines stored folded, or left with
            heavy weight on them for long periods, can take a kink that thumps until it relaxes.
            Some do relax after a few sessions; a hard crease that cracks the surface does not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A curled or frayed edge.</strong> An edge that has been rubbing on the frame
            because the belt ran off centre can curl up and slap the side rail or the roller end
            once per revolution. That is a tracking problem first; our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring a treadmill belt</a>
            covers the adjustment, and a badly damaged edge usually means the belt is on borrowed
            time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="debris" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Debris Under the Belt or Around a Roller</h2>
<p>
            Treadmills are low to the floor and the gap between belt and deck is an excellent trap.
            Coins, hair ties, small toys, pen lids, pet hair and the occasional screw from the
            machine itself all end up underneath, and a hard object carried round by the belt
            knocks each time it reaches a roller.
          </p>
          <p>
            Debris can produce a thump that is not perfectly regular, because the object moves
            around: it may knock for a few revolutions, go quiet, then return. That irregularity is
            itself a clue, since a belt defect is fixed in place and keeps strict time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Unplug, loosen the rear roller bolts a turn or two each — counting, so you can restore
            the setting — and lift the belt edge to look along the deck with a torch. Check both
            roller ends too, because hair and fibre wind tightly round the roller shafts and build
            into a lump that knocks against the frame. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-clean-under-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cleaning under a treadmill belt</a>
            walks through the access safely.
          </p>
          <p>
            If what you find is a screw or a washer, do not just throw it away. Find out where it
            came from. A fastener on the floor of a treadmill is a fastener missing from somewhere
            that is now loose, and that somewhere is often the source of the next noise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="footstrike-thump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. With Every Footstrike: Deck Bolts and Cushions</h2>
<p>
            If the thump keeps time with your feet and disappears with the belt running empty, the
            deck is moving when it should not be, or it is landing on something it should not land
            on.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck mounting bolts.</strong> The deck is bolted to the frame, usually at four
            or more points along the side rails. When one works loose, the deck can lift and drop a
            fraction of an inch with each step, and a board dropping onto a steel frame makes a
            dull, solid thump. Unplug the machine, find the deck bolts — often under the side rails
            or visible from the side — and check each is snug. Do not force them; many thread into
            nuts held in the frame or into inserts in the deck board, and over-tightening can crush
            the board or strip the insert.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cushions and isolators.</strong> Most home treadmills sit the deck on rubber or
            elastomer cushions to soften footstrike. Those cushions harden, crack, compress
            permanently or fall out of position with age. When one fails, that corner of the deck
            has less support and bottoms out against the frame under load. Look along the
            underside of the deck from the side with a torch: missing, split or flattened cushions
            are usually visible, and they are typically inexpensive parts for mainstream models.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some machines have adjustable cushioning with a selector or a set of interchangeable
            elastomers. If yours does, check that both sides are set the same. A deck that is soft
            on one side and firm on the other twists under load and can knock.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A deck that has been flipped or replaced.</strong> If the thump started after
            someone worked on the deck, assume the hardware went back differently — a missing
            washer, a cushion left out, or a bolt that went into the wrong hole. Comparing against
            the exploded diagram in the manual is the quickest check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="frame-and-floor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Loose Frame, Uneven Floor and Levelling Feet</h2>
<p>
            The next ring out from the deck is the frame, and beyond that the floor. Both produce
            footstrike-timed thumps, and the floor is the one people forget.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Levelling feet.</strong> A treadmill that rests on three feet with the fourth
            just clear of the floor rocks diagonally each time you land, and the fourth foot
            thumping down is exactly the noise you are hearing. Press down on each corner in turn
            with the machine off. Any corner that moves needs its foot wound down until the frame
            sits solidly. Then put a spirit level across the frame: rocking and tracking problems
            have the same root cause, which is why levelling also helps a belt that drifts.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame and upright bolts.</strong> The joints where the uprights meet the base,
            the folding hinge on folding machines, and the handrail fixings all take load every
            stride. A loose one can knock rather than squeak if there is enough play. Go round
            every accessible bolt with the machine unplugged, snug rather than forced. A folding
            latch that is not fully engaged deserves particular attention, because it can clunk
            with each step and it is also a safety item.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The floor itself.</strong> On a suspended timber floor — most upstairs rooms —
            the floor flexes under a running user and booms like a drum, and the thump you hear
            may be the joists rather than the machine. On thick carpet, the feet sink unevenly and
            the frame works against itself. A firm equipment mat helps in both cases, and our guide
            to
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using a treadmill on carpet</a>
            covers what to put underneath. If the thump is much louder downstairs than in the room
            itself, it is mostly transmitted noise, and the fix is isolation rather than repair.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rollers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Rollers, Bearings and the Front Pulley</h2>
<p>
            Rollers turn fast enough that their faults usually sound like a rhythmic knock or a
            throb rather than a slow thump, and the rhythm rises sharply with speed. Run the machine
            empty and listen at each end; a long screwdriver held handle to ear with the tip on the
            frame near each roller bracket is a surprisingly good stethoscope. Keep it away from
            anything moving.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A roller loose in its bracket.</strong> The rear roller is held by the tension
            bolts on each side. If they have been backed off too far, or one side has come loose,
            the roller can shift slightly with every footstrike and knock against its mounting.
            This is a footstrike-timed knock from the back of the machine, and correcting tension
            evenly usually fixes it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A failing bearing.</strong> A worn bearing with play in it knocks under load
            and rumbles when empty, loudest at the end with the bad roller. As the squeaking guide
            explains, consumer rollers usually have pressed-in sealed bearings, so the repair is a
            replacement roller rather than a bearing. The most common avoidable cause is a belt
            tensioned well past correct.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A bent roller or a loose pulley.</strong> The front roller carries a pulley for
            the motor drive belt. A pulley loose on its shaft, or a roller bent by a dropped weight
            or rough transport, wobbles once per roller revolution. You may be able to see the wobble
            at the roller end with the belt running empty at low speed. Both mean a replacement
            part, and both are worth doing promptly because the wobble loads the bearings and the
            drive belt with every turn.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-and-drive" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. The Motor, Flywheel and Drive Belt</h2>
<p>
            Under the motor hood, the drive motor turns a flywheel and a pulley, and a short ribbed
            drive belt connects it to the front roller. All three can knock, and all three are
            accessible only with the machine unplugged and the hood removed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Drive belt.</strong> A ribbed drive belt with a damaged section — a missing
            chunk, a lump, or a split — thumps once per drive-belt revolution, which is quicker
            than the running belt but slow enough to hear as individual beats at low speed. Turn
            the front roller slowly by hand with the machine unplugged and look along the full
            length of the drive belt. Cracks across the ribs are age; a missing section is a
            replacement. A drive belt that is too slack can also slap against the motor housing
            under load.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor mounting.</strong> Motors sit on brackets with bolts that can loosen, and
            a motor rocking on its mount knocks when the load changes — exactly when you plant your
            foot. Check the mounting bolts while the hood is off. Many machines tension the drive
            belt by the motor position, so note where things sit before loosening anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Flywheel.</strong> A flywheel that has picked up damage or debris, or has come
            loose on the motor shaft, produces a heavy, fast, rhythmic throb that is present with
            the belt running empty. This is not something to run on. If you can see the flywheel
            moving on the shaft or wobbling, stop and have the motor assessed.
          </p>
          <p>
            While the hood is off, look at the general condition of the compartment. A layer of
            dust and belt debris is common and worth clearing — our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">vacuuming the motor compartment</a>
            covers doing it without damaging anything. Do not touch the controller board, and if
            anything looks scorched or melted, stop there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-clunk" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. The Incline Motor Clunk</h2>
<p>
            Some clunks have nothing to do with the belt at all and happen only when the incline
            changes or when the machine is set at a grade. Changing the incline with nobody on the
            machine and the belt stopped isolates this system cleanly.
          </p>
          <p>
            A whirr while the deck is rising or lowering is normal. A single clunk at the start
            or end of travel, or a knock that repeats while the deck is moving, usually means play
            somewhere in the lift mechanism: a loose mounting bolt on the incline motor, a worn
            bushing at a pivot, or slack where the lift screw meets the frame. On some machines a
            clunk at the very bottom of travel is simply the frame settling onto its stops and is
            normal; if it has always done it, it probably is.
          </p>
          <p>
            A clunk that happens with each footstrike only when the machine is inclined is usually
            the same play being taken up by your weight. The frame is supported partly by the lift
            mechanism at a grade, and any looseness there shows up as a knock on landing. Check the
            incline motor mounting bolts and the pivot hardware with the machine unplugged and
            lowered fully.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the incline also behaves badly — stops at one grade, moves one way only, or throws
            an error — the noise is a symptom of a wider fault. Our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-incline-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill incline that is not working</a>
            covers that separately. Never put hands or feet under the deck while the incline is
            moving; the lift mechanism closes with considerable force.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="not-a-fault" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Thumps That Are Not a Fault</h2>
<p>
            Before anyone orders a part, it is worth ruling out the noises that come from use
            rather than from the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Your own footstrike.</strong> A runner who lands heavily on the heel, or who
            runs close to the front of the deck, makes a thump that no amount of maintenance will
            remove. Have someone else walk on the machine while you listen; if the thump changes
            character with the user, part of it is technique. Running nearer the middle of the deck
            and with a slightly quicker cadence usually quietens it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Loose items.</strong> A phone, a water bottle, a tablet holder or the safety
            key clip rattling on the console makes a convincing knock that seems to come from deep
            inside the frame, because steel carries sound. Clear the console and the cup holders and
            listen again.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A cushioned deck doing its job.</strong> Decks that are designed to flex make
            some noise as they move. If the machine is new, the noise has been there since day one,
            it is not changing, and every bolt is tight, it may simply be the design. The question
            to ask is whether it has changed; a new or worsening thump is a fault until shown
            otherwise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-stop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. When a Thump Means Stop, and What Repairs Cost</h2>
<p>
            Most thumps are worth fixing within the week rather than within the hour. A few are
            not, and they deserve stating plainly.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>A belt that is visibly delaminating, bulging, or tearing at the seam.</li>
            <li>A thump accompanied by the belt hesitating, jerking or slipping under your foot.</li>
            <li>A deck that moves visibly when you step on it, or a folding latch that is not secure.</li>
            <li>A heavy throb from the motor compartment, a flywheel that wobbles, or any burning smell — see our <a href="/treadmill-burning-smell/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">burning smell guide</a>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Any of those means stop using the machine until it is fixed. The others — a stiff seam,
            a loose foot, a tired cushion — are worth fixing promptly because impacts load
            everything around them, but they are not emergencies.
          </p>
          <p>
            As for cost, most of the fixes on this page are free: tightening, levelling, clearing
            debris and correcting tension. The parts that do cost money vary widely by model, so
            treat these as typical ranges rather than quotes: deck cushions and isolators are
            usually inexpensive; a drive belt is commonly a modest part cost; a replacement running
            belt typically runs from around $80 to $250; and a roller somewhere in a similar band.
            Professional labour adds to all of them, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost guide</a>
            covers when a repair stops making sense against the value of the machine. If you are
            still not sure where the noise is coming from, the wider
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>
            sorts faults by symptom.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my treadmill make a thumping noise every few seconds?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A slow, regular thump with nobody on the machine is almost always the running belt — usually a stiff or lifting seam, a bulge from delamination, or something stuck to the underside — because the belt is the slowest-turning part. Mark the belt with tape, run it at low speed, and see whether the thump arrives each time the mark passes a roller.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my treadmill thump when I walk on it but not when it runs empty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A thump that keeps time with your footstrike and disappears with the belt running empty is structural. The usual causes are loose deck mounting bolts, hardened or missing deck cushions, a loose frame or folding joint, or a levelling foot that is not touching the floor so the frame rocks on every step.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a treadmill belt seam supposed to make noise?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A new belt can have a slightly noticeable seam for its first few hours, which usually settles. On an older belt, a seam that thumps has usually stiffened or started to lift, and it tends to get worse rather than better. A seam that is visibly separating means the belt should be replaced. Over-tension can also make a seam noisier, so check tension first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can debris under the belt cause a knocking noise?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is common. Coins, hair ties, small toys and stray screws get carried round under the belt and knock as they reach a roller. Debris often gives a slightly irregular knock because the object moves. Unplug the machine, slacken the belt by counted turns, look along the deck with a torch, and check the roller ends for wound-on hair and fibre.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my treadmill clunk when the incline changes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A whirr during incline travel is normal. A clunk at the start or end of travel, or a knock while the deck moves, usually means play in the lift mechanism — a loose incline motor mounting bolt, a worn pivot bushing, or slack at the lift screw. If the incline also stops at one level or throws an error, treat it as an incline fault rather than a noise.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to keep using a treadmill that is thumping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often for a short while, but not always. Stop if the belt is delaminating or tearing, the belt hesitates or jerks under your foot, the deck visibly moves when you step on it, a folding latch is not secure, or there is a heavy throb from the motor or any burning smell. Other thumps should still be fixed promptly because impacts load the surrounding parts.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Chasing a Thump</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most wasted money on treadmill noises comes from skipping the timing test and going straight to a part. These four are the usual ways it happens.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a thump like a squeak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Lubricating the deck is the right answer to a dry, rubbing belt and the wrong answer to an impact. A thump is something hitting something on a schedule; silicone will not quieten a lifting seam, a loose deck bolt or a rocking foot, and too much of it can make a belt slip.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering a belt before checking tension</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A belt tightened well past correct bends its seam harder over the rollers, loads the bearings and can create the very thump that makes people think the belt is finished. The lift test takes thirty seconds and costs nothing. Do it before buying anything.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A levelling foot clear of the floor makes the frame rock and thump on every step, and an upstairs timber floor can boom like a drum under a running user. Press each corner, level the frame and consider a firm mat before taking the machine apart.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Over-tightening deck bolts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">When a deck thumps, the instinct is to crank its bolts down hard. Many thread into inserts in the board or into captive nuts, and forcing them crushes the deck or strips the thread — turning a loose bolt into a deck repair. Snug, evenly, and check the cushions instead.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

