        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Time it before you touch it.</strong> A slow thump every few
            seconds with the belt running empty is the belt — usually the seam, a bulge or something
            stuck underneath. A thump on every footstrike is the deck, its cushions, the frame or
            the floor. A fast knock or throb is a roller, the drive belt or the motor.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Start with the free fixes: tension, levelling, debris and bolts. Stop using the machine if
            the belt is delaminating or the deck moves underfoot, and if the noise is a high-pitched
            rub rather than an impact, the
            <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">squeaking noise guide</a>
            is the better place to start.
          </p>
